  ## What to do

  1. Download the latest upstream config:

       https://github.com/SwiftOnSecurity/sysmon-config/blob/master/sysmonconfig-export.xml

     (Direct raw link:
      https://raw.githubusercontent.com/SwiftOnSecurity/sysmon-config/master/sysmonconfig-export.xml)

  2. Save it as `setup/sysmon-config.xml` in this repo, overwriting this stub.

  3. Install / update Sysmon on the Windows victim with an elevated PowerShell:

       .\Sysmon64.exe -accepteula -i sysmon-config.xml      # first install
       .\Sysmon64.exe -c sysmon-config.xml                   # update config

  4. Confirm the service is running and populating the Operational channel:

       Get-Service sysmon64
       Get-WinEvent -LogName 'Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational' -MaxEvents 5

  ## Why SwiftOnSecurity's config

  It's the de facto community baseline: broad process / network / image-load
  coverage, sensible exclusions for common Windows noise, and active
  maintenance. Olaf Hartong's `sysmon-modular` is a more advanced alternative
  (rule-by-rule composition) and is an obvious upgrade path once this lab has
  real detection volume to tune against.

